	We already have it implemented in code :D

EXEC_OPS()
    Traverse all tokens
	    1. Take i-th token
		2. IF this i-th token is operand-program
		3. Let's see the next token after i-th
		    3.1. IF the next token (i-th + 1) is && or ||
			    3.1.1. Expand and assign variables
				3.1.2. Update quotes
			    3.1.3. Expand wildcards
				3.1.4. Update quotes
				3.1.5. Update redirections (after path and variables expansion new redirection-operands may appear)
				3.1.6. Determine program's name and its arguments
				3.1.7. IF program's name contains /
				    3.1.7.1. Launch execve withtout changing program's name
				3.1.8. ELSE (does not contain /)
				    3.1.8.1. IF program is built-in
					    3.1.8.1.1. Run built-in
						3.1.8.1.2. Go to 3.1.11
					3.1.8.2. ELSE (not built-in)
					    3.1.8.2.2. Search in PATH
				3.1.8.  Do fork() for this operand-program
				3.1.9.  Assign redirections and heredocs (open all files and bound their FDs with program STDIN/STDOUT using dup())
				3.1.10. Do execve() for this program passing to it parsed arguments and environment
				3.1.11. Wait until program will be finished
				3.1.12. Get this program's returned value
				3.1.13. IF the program's returned value is SUCCESS

				    3.1.13.1. IF the next token is && (and returned value is SUCCESS)
						3.1.13.1.1. IF the next token after && (i-th + 2) is operand
						    3.1.13.1.1.1. Move to this token
						    3.1.13.1.1.2. Go to step 3.1.1
					    3.1.13.1.2. IF the next token after && (i-th + 2) is '('
						    3.1.13.1.2.1. Launch a subshell
							3.1.13.1.2.2. Call EXEC_OPS() in the subshell

					3.1.13.2. IF the next token is || (and returned value is SUCCESS) 
						3.1.13.2.1. IF the next token after || (i-th + 2) is operand
						    3.1.13.2.1.1. Skip this || and this next (i-th + 2) operand
						    3.1.13.2.1.2. Continue
					    3.1.13.2.2. IF the next token after || (i-th + 2) is '('
						    3.1.13.2.2.1. Skip all inside this parentheses block
							3.1.13.2.2.2. Continue

				3.1.14. IF the program's returned value is FAILURE
		
				    3.1.14.1. IF the next token is && (and returned value is FAILURE)
						3.1.14.1.1. IF the next token after && (i-th + 2) is operand
						    3.1.14.2.1.1. Skip this && and this next (i-th + 2) operand
						    3.1.14.2.1.2. Continue
					    3.1.14.1.2. IF the next token after && (i-th + 2) is '('
						    3.1.14.2.2.1. Skip all inside this parentheses block
							3.1.14.2.2.2. Continue

					3.1.14.2. IF the next token is || (and returned value is FAILURE) 
						3.1.14.2.1. IF the next token after || (i-th + 2) is operand
						    3.1.14.1.1.1. Move to this token
						    3.1.14.1.1.2. Go to step 3.1.1
					    3.1.14.2.2. IF the next token after || (i-th + 2) is '('
						    341.14.1.2.1. Launch a subshell
							3.1.14.1.2.2. Call EXEC_OPS() in the subshell

		    3.2. IF the next token (i-th + 1) is | (we have to reach the end of this pipeline meanwhile connecting its componenets together via pipes)
			    3.2.1. Go one token forward
			    3.2.2. LOOP until we reach the end of tokens' array

					3.2.2.1. IF the current token is |
						3.2.2.1.1. Create a new pipe using pipe()
						3.2.2.1.2. Add created pipe in the array of all pipes
						3.2.2.1.3. IF this is the first pipe in the current pipeline
							3.2.2.1.3.1. Assign to 'STDIN' (read-end) of the last operand-program found the NONE_PIPE value (-1)
						3.2.2.1.4. ELSE (not the first pipe)
							3.2.2.1.4.1. Assign 'STDIN' (read-end) of the last operand-program found the index of the second to last pipe in pipes array
						3.2.2.1.5. Assign 'STDOUT' (write-end) of the last operand-program the index of the last created pipe

					3.2.2.2. IF the current token is an operand-program
						3.2.2.2.1. Mark this operand-program as the last operand-program found

					3.2.2.3. IF the current token is a logical operator (this means we reached the end of the current pipeline)
						3.2.2.3.1. Assign 'STDOUT' (write-end) of the last operand-program found the NONE_PIPE value (-1)
						3.2.2.3.2. Remember the index of the last token in the pipeline
						3.2.2.3.3. Break

					3.2.2.4. IF the current token is '(' (in this case it's guaranteed the previous token was a pipe)
						3.2.2.4.1. Assign 'STDIN' (read-end) of this opening-parenthesis the index of the last pipe found
						3.2.2.4.2. Skip this entire parentheses block and move ourselves onto the first token that goes after ')'
						3.2.2.4.3. IF there is no any token after ')' (it means the ')' was the last token)
						    3.2.2.4.3.1. Assign 'STDOUT' (write-end) of this opening-parenthesis the NONE_PIPE value (-1)
						3.2.2.4.4. ELSE (not the last token; in fact after ')' may go only logical operator or a pipe)
						    3.2.2.4.4.1. Go to the next token

						3.2.2.4.3. Assign 'STDOUT' (write-end) of this opening-parenthesis the 

					3.2.2.5. Go to the next token

				3.2.3. END of LOOP until we reach the end of tokens' array
				# We're at the i-th token (with which our pipelines begins). Now we're gonna execute all operand-programs in this pipeline
				3.2.4. Extract the index of the last token in the pipeline
				3.2.5. LOOP until we reach the extracted token index
					3.2.5.1. IF the current token is |
					    3.2.5.1.1. Skip it
					3.2.5.2. IF the current token is '('
					    3.2.5.2.1. Launch the subshell and call EXEC_OPS() into it
					3.2.5.3. IF the current token is operand-program
						3.2.5.3.1. Expand wildcards
						3.2.5.3.2. Expand and assign variables
						3.2.5.3.3. Determine program name and its arguments
						3.2.5.3.4. Parse redirections and heredocs
						3.2.5.3.5. Do fork() for this operand-program
						3.2.5.3.6. Assign redirections and heredocs (open all files and bound their FDs with program STDIN/STDOUT using dup())
						3.2.5.3.7. Do execve() for this program passing to it parsed arguments and environment
					3.2.5.4. Next operand
				3.2.6. END of LOOP until we reach the extracted token index (we launched all operand-programs in the processed pipeline)
				3.2.6. Wait for all launched operand-programs in the processed pipeline
				3.2.7. Get the returned code of the last operand-program executed in the processed pipeline
				
